    13	## 1. What the book asks, made precise
    14	
    15	N identical spin-f bosons in one spatial mode (or the spin factor of a symmetric state) carry the
    16	symmetric representation [N] of U(2f+1). Total-spin eigenstates |N; Γ L M⟩ are labelled by
    17	L (total spin) and extra labels Γ. The **one-body CFP** is the coefficient in
    18	
    19	  |N; Γ L M⟩ = Σ_{Γ' L'} ⟨N Γ L {| N−1 Γ' L'⟩ [χ(particle N) ⊗ |N−1; Γ' L'⟩]_{L M},
    20	
    21	and two-body CFPs are defined in the same way with a coupled pair. The book says (i) no general
    22	analytic formula exists; CFPs come from a recursion in N that becomes unmanageable for 10–1000
    23	particles; (ii) only the spin-1 boson case has a closed form; (iii) other cases are "very hard".
    24	"Cracking the recursion" therefore means a closed form valid for **all N**, with no step-by-step
    25	recursion.
    26	
    27	For spin f ≥ 2 the natural labels are seniority v (the irrep (v) of O(2f+1)) and an extra
    28	multiplicity label α for repeated L inside (v): U(2f+1) ⊃ O(2f+1) ⊃ SO(3). **The missing label α is
    29	the core difficulty.** For f = 2 (d-bosons, spin-2 condensates) the first multiplicity occurs at
    30	v = 6, L = 6.
