    42	
    43	## A. Theorem A — the N-recursion is solved for every spin f
    44	
    45	**Theorem A.** Let N = v + 2n, D = 2f+1, and let |N vαL⟩, |N−1 v'α'L'⟩ be built as in §0
    46	from orthonormal harmonic states. Then c = 0 unless v' = v ± 1, and
    47	
    48	  c(N vαL; N−1, v−1, α'L') = √[ v(N+v+D−2) / (N(2v+D−2)) ] · r(vαL; v−1 α'L'),
    49	
    50	  c(N vαL; N−1, v+1, α'L') = (−1)^{L+L'} √[ (N−v)(v+D−2) / (N(2v+D−2)) ]
    51	          · √[ dim_v (2L'+1) / (dim_{v+1}(2L+1)) ] · r(v+1 α'L'; vαL),
    52	
    53	where dim_v = (2v+D−2)(v+D−3)!/(v!(D−2)!) is the dimension of the O(D) irrep (v).
    54	
    55	*Proof.* (i) P(P†)^n h = 4n(v + D/2 + n − 1)(P†)^{n−1}h for harmonic h of degree v (induction
    56	on n with [P,P†] = 4N̂+2D). Hence ‖(P†)^n h‖² = c_{n,v}‖h‖², c_{n,v} = Π_{k=1}^{n} 4k(v+D/2+k−1).
    57	States with different v are orthogonal (eigenvectors of P†P with distinct eigenvalues).
    58	(ii) Write Φ = [a† ⊗ h']_{LM} for harmonic h' of degree v−1. Then PΦ = 2[ã ⊗ h']_{LM} with
    59	ã_m = (−1)^m a_{−m}, using [P, a†_m] = 2(−1)^m a_{−m}. This vector is harmonic, so
    60	Φ = h_v + P†g with g = [ã⊗h']/(2v+D−4) and h_v harmonic of degree v.
    61	(iii) v' = v−1: [a† ⊗ (P†)^n h'] = (P†)^n Φ, because a† commutes with P†. Only (P†)^n h_v overlaps
    62	the parent, so c = N^{−1/2} √(c_{n,v}/c_{n,v−1}) ⟨h|Φ⟩. Telescoping gives
    63	c_{n,v}/c_{n,v−1} = (N+v+D−2)/(2v+D−2), and ⟨h|Φ⟩ = √v·r.
    64	(iv) v' = v+1: the daughter is (P†)^{n−1}h''/√c_{n−1,v+1} with h'' harmonic of degree v+1. By (ii),
    65	[a† ⊗ h''] = h_{v+2} + P†[ã ⊗ h'']/(2v+D), and only the second term overlaps (P†)^n h. Hence
    66	c = N^{−1/2} c_{n,v}/√(c_{n,v} c_{n−1,v+1}) · ⟨h|[ã⊗h'']_L⟩/(2v+D), with
    67	c_{n,v}/c_{n−1,v+1} = 2n(2v+D). The CG identity
    68	⟨f −m; L L|L' L−m⟩ = (−1)^{m+L'−L} √((2L'+1)/(2L+1)) ⟨f m; L' L−m|L L⟩ gives
    69	⟨h|[ã⊗h'']_L⟩ = (−1)^{L'−L} √((2L'+1)/(2L+1)) √(v+1) r(v+1 α'L'; vαL). Collecting factors gives
    70	c² = (N−v)(v+1)(2L'+1) r²/(N(2v+D)(2L+1)). This equals the stated form because
    71	dim_v/dim_{v+1} = (v+1)(2v+D−2)/((2v+D)(v+D−2)). ∎
    72	
    73	All N-dependence is therefore closed-form. The whole problem reduces to the N-independent
    74	O(2f+1) ⊃ SO(3) isoscalar factors r at N = v.
    75	
    76	**Corollary A1 (f = 1, Bao–Li).** For f = 1 the irrep (v) of O(3) is spin L = v. There is one
    77	daughter, r = 1, and the dimension ratio is 1. Theorem A gives a_S² = (N−S)(S+1)/(N(2S+1)) and
    78	b_S² = (N+S+1)S/(N(2S+1)). In our pair-operator phase the S+1 branch has sign (−1)^{L+L'} = −1.
    79	With the Clebsch–Gordan singlet pair Σ(−1)^{f−m}a†_m a†_{−m}, as used by Bao & Li, both are positive.
    80	
    81	*Verification (ran):* `cd code && $PY check_seniority_factorization.py 30` printed
    82	`spin-1 Bao-Li closed form: 465 CFPs equal exactly (N <= 30)` and, for f = 1, 2, 3, 4 with
    83	N ≤ 16, 12, 9, 7: `Theorem A verified exactly for all 81 / 358 / 594 / 566 seniority states …
    84	136 / 4492 / 21513 / 26349 CFPs, completeness sum = 1; sign rule (-1)^(L+L') holds for 15 / 519 /
    85	3124 / 3653 v+1 couplings`.
