   166	## D. Theorem D — Bao–Li-type closed forms are impossible in multiplicity families (rational bases)
   167	
   168	Setting: let V_k be the two-dimensional space of seniority-v, spin-(2v−k) states, k ∈ {6, 8, 9, 10, 11},
   169	over K = Q(v). It is spanned by φ₀, φ₁, the projected covariants with e = 0, 1. Let q = (Fock norm)/(v−k)!.
   170	For x ∈ V_k and a multiplicity-free daughter d with normalized state y_d,
   171	c(x/|x|; d)² = b_d(x)² C_d / q(x), where b_d(x) = ⟨x, z_μ y_d⟩ and
   172	C_d = (v−k)!/(v−1−k')! / (w_μ ‖y_d‖² v CG²). The script computes and factors C_d exactly; **every
   173	C_d is a product of linear factors**.
   174	
   175	Call c² **Bao–Li type over T** (T ⊂ Q) if c² = R(v)²·κ·Π_{t∈T}(v−t)^{n_t} with R ∈ Q(v), κ ∈ Q*
   176	and n_t ∈ Z. Take T = {n/4 : |n| ≤ 10⁶} ∪ {p/q : |p/q| ≤ 30, 1 ≤ q ≤ 30}. Every closed form in
   177	§B–§C and in Pan et al. uses only linear factors from this set.
   178	
   179	**Theorem D.** Let k ∈ {6, 8, 9, 10, 11}. For every nonzero x ∈ V_k and every multiplicity-free
   180	daughter d with b_d(x) ≠ 0, the squared CFP c(x/|x|; d)² is **not** Bao–Li type over T.
   181	Consequently every orthonormal basis of V_k made of normalized Q(v)-rational vectors has, in at
   182	least one basis vector, a nonzero CFP into a multiplicity-free daughter that is not of Bao–Li type.
   183	For k = 6 the trio-first basis of §C is **optimal**: each of its CFPs into the multiplicity-free
   184	daughters has exactly one non-linear irreducible factor, of the minimal possible degree 2.
   185	
   186	*Proof.* Because C_d is Bao–Li type, c² is Bao–Li type over T iff q(x) ∈ κ·Π(v−t)^{n_t}·K*², that
   187	is, iff q represents an element c of the group L_T. Diagonalizing,
   188	q ≅ ⟨q₁₁, q₁₁Δ⟩ with q₁₁ = q(φ₁) and Δ = det G. Then q represents c iff ⟨1, Δ, −cq₁₁⟩ is
   189	isotropic, iff the quaternion algebra (cq₁₁, −Δ) splits over K. A necessary condition is that
   190	every residue (tame symbol) vanishes.
   191	(1) *Non-linear places.* For each irreducible non-linear π dividing q₁₁ to odd order, the residue
   192	is −Δ mod π. It is a square in Q[v]/(π) in every case: an exact factorization over Q(√·) for
   193	quadratic π, and a resultant certificate for cubic π. So these places impose no condition.
   194	(2) *Rational places.* Tame symbols are bimultiplicative, so the residues of (c₀Π_{s∈S}(v−s)q₁₁, −Δ)
   195	at the rational places R_k are explicit classes in Q*/Q*². R_k consists of all rational roots of
   196	q₁₁ and Δ, plus ∞. At places where Δ has odd valuation a constant c₀ contributes c₀; elsewhere it
   197	contributes nothing. Exhausting all subsets S ⊆ R_k (2⁹ to 2¹⁵ cases) finds **no** S and c₀ for
   198	which all residues vanish.
   199	(3) *Other places.* If c has odd valuation at a place t ∈ T outside R_k, the residue there is
   200	−Δ(t). It must be a nonzero rational square. An exact integer search over T finds no such t for
   201	k ≤ 10. For k = 11 it finds t = 2, which was added to step (2); the result is still no solution.
   202	Hence q represents no c ∈ L_T.
   203	For the optimality statement: in the trio-first basis, q(u₁) = q₁₁ and q(u₂) = Δ/q₁₁. Both have
   204	square class (linear factors)·q₂(v), and q₂ = 18v²+91v−190 is irreducible (discriminant 21961 is not
   205	a square). By the theorem some non-linear factor must appear, and a non-linear factor has degree ≥ 2. ∎
   206	
   207	*Checks.* The inputs q₁₁ and Δ are bound to the symbolic derivation (assertion). The
   208	scale-invariant ratio q₀₁²/(q₀₀q₁₁) agrees with the independent numeric engine for 10 values of v
   209	per k. Weil reciprocity (the product of all residues is a square) holds. A positive control,
   210	replacing q₁₁ by the linear-type 3(v−r₁)(v−r₂), is found. The pairing matrix (b_d) has rank 2 for
   211	k = 6, 8, 9 and rank 1 for k = 10, 11, where only one multiplicity-free daughter (k' = 7) exists.
   212	*Ran:* `$PY multiplicity_obstruction.py 6` (and 8, 9, 10, 11), output in
   213	[results/k6_square_class.txt](results/k6_square_class.txt) etc. The loop
   214	`for k in 6 8 9 10 11; do $PY brauer_residues.py $k 30; done` printed
   215	`subsets S … with all residues trivial for some c0: 0 []` for all five k; see
   216	[results/brauer_residues_k6_to_k11.txt](results/brauer_residues_k6_to_k11.txt).
   217	
   218	*Scope and limits.* (a) Only **Q(v)-rational** bases are covered. A basis whose coefficients
   219	involve square roots of rational functions of v is not excluded. (b) Linear factors outside T are
   220	excluded only if the genus ≥ 4 hyperelliptic curve w² = −Δ_k(s) has no further rational points.
   221	We did not prove this; the search bounds are stated above. (c) Closed forms that are not of Bao–Li
   222	type always exist (§C): rational functions of v, containing an irreducible polynomial.
   223	
   224	## E. Low total spin, arbitrary seniority (the regime of antiferromagnetic/cyclic spin-2 condensates)
   225	
   226	**Theorem E1 (any f).** Let φ be any normalized seniority-(v−1) state with total spin 0. Then
   227	Σ_α c(v α L=f; φ)² = (2v+D−2)(v+D−3) / (D v (2v+D−4)). For f = 2 and v ≡ 1 (mod 3) the parent
   228	(v, L=2) is unique, so this is the CFP itself: r² = (v+2)(2v+3)/(5v(2v+1)).
   229	
   230	*Proof.* Let Φ = a†_f φ = [a†⊗φ]_{f f}. Rotational invariance of φ gives ⟨n_m⟩ = (v−1)/D for every
   231	m, so ‖Φ‖² = 1 + (v−1)/D. Also PΦ = 2(−1)^f a_{−f}φ is harmonic of degree v−2, so the non-harmonic
   232	part of Φ has norm² ‖PΦ‖²/(4(v−2)+2D) = 2(v−1)/(D(2v+D−4)). The harmonic part of Φ is a spin-f,
   233	seniority-v vector, so Σ_α |⟨vα f f|Φ⟩|² = ‖h_v‖². Dividing by v gives the claim. ∎
   234	*Ran:* `$PY check_general_f.py` printed `Theorem E1 (scalar-parent weight) agrees exactly: 19
   235	daughters, f = 1..4, v <= 9`.
   236	
