    11	
    12	## The problem in one paragraph
    13	
    14	Coefficients of fractional parentage (CFPs) expand a symmetric N-particle state into one
    15	particle (or a pair) times states of the remaining N−1 (N−2) particles. With them, one- and
    16	two-body matrix elements become simple sums. The book points out that there is no general
    17	analytic formula. CFPs are generated by a recursion in N that becomes unmanageable for the
    18	mesoscopic range N ≈ 10–1000. Only spin-1 bosons have a closed form (Bao & Li 2005; the j = 1
    19	result is older, Marić & Popović-Božić 1980). Other cases are called "very hard". The goal is to
    20	crack the recursion beyond spin 1.
    21	
    22	## Precise formulations attacked (and what they do not settle)
    23	
    24	For N spin-f bosons in total-spin eigenstates labelled by seniority v (the O(2f+1) irrep), a
    25	missing label α, and total spin L:
    26	
    27	1. **The N-recursion, every f.** Closed-form N-dependence of all one-body CFPs (Theorem A).
    28	2. **Infinite families as functions of v.** These are
    29	   (a) near-stretched states L = fv − 2, fv − 3 for every f (Theorem B), and the first
    30	       multiplicity family L = fv − 4 for every f ≥ 3 (Theorem B');
    31	   (b) spin 2, L = 2v − k for k ≤ 8 (Claim C);
    32	   (c) spin 2, low total spin L ≤ 5 at any seniority (Theorem E1, Conjecture E2).
    33	3. **The multiplicity question.** Can the missing label be chosen so that CFPs keep the Bao–Li
    34	   product form? For spin 2: no rational basis does it in the first five multiplicity-2 families
    35	   (Theorem D). For every spin f ≥ 3: yes, the pair-first basis does it in the first
    36	   multiplicity family (Theorem B').
    37	
    38	**Not settled:** a uniform closed form for every (v, α, L) of spin 2; general f beyond the
    39	near-stretched families; spin-3/2 fermions (not attacked); a proof of Conjecture E2. Two-body CFPs
    40	follow from the one-body ones by the standard chain formula, which is verified exactly
    41	(RESULTS Claim G).
